Skip a custom gradient with no stop in the writer

The config dialog can take the last stop out of a custom gradient. The
gradient then keeps its entry in the option set with an empty stop
container. The writer holds no test for that state, so it emits the name
of the border alone.

The reader takes no such value. It drops the gradient of every key that
holds a value. It rebuilds the gradient only from a value of five
elements at least. The border of the saved gradient therefore does not
come back. The file also carries a key that no read ever uses.

Write no key for a gradient with no stop. The write then matches what
the reader takes. A gradient with one stop or more keeps its exact text.
The container adds the end stops, so the value holds two stops at least.

An absent key means the built-in gradient. The two gradients that the
defaults define therefore come back with their built-in stops after a
save and a load. The empty state that the discarded key left behind does
not come back.

The change sits on the branch work/config-io of the local tree, as commit 18 of 18.
A new merge request on system/qtcurve takes that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

diff --git a/qt6/common/config_file.cpp b/qt6/common/config_file.cpp
index f97bad21..fa6bf8f0 100644
--- a/qt6/common/config_file.cpp
+++ b/qt6/common/config_file.cpp
@@ -2566,7 +2566,9 @@ bool qtcWriteConfig(KConfig *cfg, const Options &opts, const Options &def, bool
 
             QTextStream(&gradKey) << "customgradient" << i - APPEARANCE_CUSTOM1 + 1;
 
-            if (cg == opts.customGradient.end()) {
+            // A value of this key holds two stops at least, so a gradient
+            // with no stop takes no value.
+            if (cg == opts.customGradient.end() || (*cg).second.stops.empty()) {
                 CFG.deleteEntry(gradKey);
             }
             else {
